            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;



Overall, your house is relatively tight, however it will become a bit tighter when you:



- Upgrade the boiler, which will eliminate the current chimney &amp; open combustion air supply pipe.



- Perhaps install a dedicated combustion air supply pipe for the woodstove.



- Perhaps replace the existing windows with new ESTAR units.



If you also upgrade the HRV to a new ESTAR unit that operates at high efficiency, this additional air tightness of the home will be managed.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a modest 10% reduction in air leakage, there would not be much in annual energy savings, however you may just qualify (if the air leakage reduction is a minimum 10%) for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a second home energy evaluation, where the air leakage reduction will be measured. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</airSeal>
                <mainWalls />
                <ceilings />
                <exposedFloors />
                <foundation />
                <windows>

There are a lot of windows in your house &amp; many of them are original to the house in 1985, although some second floor units appear to have had the thermopanes updated. All of the original windows have no Low E coating &amp; therefore no argon gas fill. The replaced thermopanes on the upper floor have a weak Low E coating, so likely no argon gas fill as well.



Many of the windows have an interior plastic film applied to reduce air leakage or trap another layer of air for insulation purposes. During the blower door test, I did not see these plastic window films bulging in significantly, so I would say the plastic film is there more for insulation purposes than to reduce air leakage. They are wood windows, so maintenance of the wood is an ongoing issue you will deal with.



For your longer term energy efficiency planning, I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading all of these older windows (and the glass patio door into the sunroom) to ENERGY STARÂ® qualified units rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing all of the older windows (and the glass patio door) with ENERGY STARÂ® qualified units rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 881&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;24.3 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</windows>
                <VentilationSystem>

During my evaluation of your home, it appears the current Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) in your house is an older unit that is not very efficient and may be on a manufacturer recall list (as per the info I emailed you).



Proper ventilation protects a home from unwanted toxins, pollutants, and odors and can also help protect the overall structure of a home or building by eliminating excess moisture from the air. An energy-efficient heat recovery ventilator is one of the best ways to control indoor air quality, and we would recommend a minimum efficiency of 65% (although there are many available that operate at 70% - 75%. 



I would recommend that you replace your current HRV with an ENERGY STARÂ® rated HRV system (minimum 65% efficiency) designed and installed by an individual who holds current certification from an organization, such as HRAI.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing your current HRV with a 65% efficient ENERGY STARÂ® rated system, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 53 &lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;1.5 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing an HRV.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>

The current boiler in your home has an integrated domestic hot water loop that also heats the hot water for the house, with a 182 litre electric hot water tank integrated for a buffer of hot water storage. My upgrade recommendation for this is tied to the boiler upgrade ......... so please review that section of my report.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current oil boiler has an estimated efficiency of 83% Annual Fuel Utilization Efficiency (AFUE), and has a 6" chimney that vents up through the 2 floors of the house and there is a 5" open combustion air pipe that provides combustion air for the boiler. The boiler also has an integrated domestic hot water loop that heats the hot water for the house, with a 182 litre electric hot water tank integrated for a buffer of hot water storage. This type of setup is estimated to operate at a 0.56 Energy Factor (EF) for the hot water portion. During the non-heating season, the boiler can be turned off so the hot water needs of the house will be supplied by the electric hot water tank solely ..... that will prevent short-cycling of the oil boiler.



I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. This new boiler would continue to supply the domestic hot water and can be used year round as the new boiler would be modulating to adjust to the combined heating &amp; hot water needs. The efficiency of the domestic hot water portion would increase to 0.79 EF.



There are some side benefits to this type of system as the current chimney &amp; combustion air supply pipe will be eliminated, as the new boiler would be direct vented to the outside of the house with a twin pipe system.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your current oil boiler with a high-efficiency condensing propane unit with an efficiency of 95% AFUE (eliminating the current chimney &amp; combustion air pepe) could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1,479&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;32.9 GJ&lt;/b&gt;., and you would qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. In addition, there is another estimated &lt;b&gt;$ 170 of savings&lt;/b&gt; per year for the increased efficiency of the domestic hot water, and decrease your annual energy usage by another &lt;b&gt;3.6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables />
                <AdditionalComments>

&lt;b&gt;Combustion Air for the Wood Stove&lt;/b&gt;



The current wood stove vents up 2 storey through the cathedral ceiling, and this wood stove draws its' combustion air from inside the house. This means, it draws your interior heated air for combustion &amp; then vents it up the chimney. Even when the wood stove is not being used, there will be some stack effect heat loss up through the chimney. The Arctic Energy Alliance always recommends that wood &amp; pellet stoves have a dedicated outside combustion air sealed to the stove. For your Blaze King, there is a hood at the rear bottom of the stove to have this connected.



I have modeled the estimated energy savings (in addition to the new propane boiler) if you were to have a dedicated outside combustion air pipe connected to the wood stove.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

A dedicated (direct connect &amp; sealed) outside combustion air connected to the wood stove could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 73&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs. 



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Ventilation&lt;/b&gt;



Your home is currently relatively air tight (ie: not alot of natural air leakage through the building envelope) and includes the following mechanical ventilation appliances:

        - a heat recover ventilator (HRV) - was not operating at the time of my visit

        - three bathroom exhaust fans - should upgrade to ESTAR &amp; have timer switches installed

        - a clothes dryer that exhausts to the exterior - the vent was heavily frosted over

        - the kitchen range hood fan does not exhaust to the outside - maybe vent to outside if desired



The bathroom fans should be upgrading to ENERGY STAR rated units, that seal better, use much less electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



The current HRV is older and not very efficient. From the email I sent you, it may also be part of a safety recall by the manufacturer, that you should look into further. There is a section of this report that outlines an upgrade option to a new ENERGY STAR rated HRV, as your house will require some addition high efficiency mechanical ventilation to manage indoor air quality &amp; humidity.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Heating Zones for Crawlspace &amp; Main Floor&lt;/b&gt;



During my evaluation of your home, we looked at the in-floor heating of the main floor, that also doubles as a heat source for the lower crawlspace. I think there is a better way to control the heat for both levels.



You should investigate a better in-floor heat loop system for the main floor &amp; have adequate insulation (and heat shields) below the loops to direct the heat to the main floor (controlled by the main floor thermostat). You could then use the existing heat loops (aluminum fin) to operate in a loop for the crawlspace and controlled by a separate thermostat as its' own zone, and set lower than the main floor.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Depressurization Test&lt;/b&gt;



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the 3 bathroom fans, and the dryer, indicated a pressure drop of 3.2 Pascals. As this number is lower than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current boiler is a chimney vented model, as is the wood stove. The upgrade recommendations for a new condensing propane boiler &amp; installing a dedicated combustion air for the wood stove will eliminate this potential risk moving forward.



During my assessment of your home, there appeared to be functioning smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Yellowknife&lt;/b&gt;



The following Nov. 1, 2018 values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $18.70 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.29 per litre 

Propane - $0.80 per litre

Wood - $341 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.51 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



